# AI AGENTS: READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING ANYTHING

> **This machine is shared. Several AI agents may be working on it AT THE SAME TIME as you, and you
> cannot see them.**

## Mandatory protocol: measure pressure first, then run heavy work through `heavy`

1. **Before any heavy command, run `pressure`.**
   - `GREEN` (exit 0): OK, start it (still through `heavy`, step 2).
   - `YELLOW` (exit 1): light work only. Queue heavy work with `heavy <cmd>`; it waits its turn.
   - `RED` (exit 2): **do not start builds, test suites, installs or browsers.** Run `pressure --wait`
     (blocks until GREEN) or do light work meanwhile.
2. **Run every heavy command through `heavy`**, e.g. `heavy npm test`, `heavy uv run pytest ...`.
   `heavy` allows at most @SLOTS@ heavy job(s) machine-wide; extra jobs queue and run at low priority.
   `heavy --status` shows what is running and from which directory.
3. **Never fan out test suites or builds yourself**: no `&`-backgrounding several suites, no
   sub-agents that each run a full build or suite.
4. **While iterating, run the single relevant test file**, not the whole suite.
5. Cap worker counts to this machine's @THREADS@ threads.

**Heavy** = builds, full test suites, whole-project type checks or lint, big installs, Docker builds,
browser tests, compiles, data processing. **Light** (no `heavy` needed) = editing, git, `rg`/`fdfind`,
reading logs, one small test file, short scripts.

If `pressure`/`heavy` are not on PATH, use `~/.local/bin/pressure` and `~/.local/bin/heavy`.

