## Capacity rules

- Keep at least @MIN_FREE_GIB@ GiB RAM available; `pressure` turns YELLOW under @YELLOW_FREE_GIB@ GiB and
  RED under @MIN_FREE_GIB@ GiB, at a load above @LOAD_YELLOW@ (YELLOW) or @LOAD_RED@ (RED).
- Heavy work: at most @SLOTS@ at a time (what `heavy` enforces).
- Rough sizes: an agent CLI session 0.3-0.6 GiB; a large build 1-3 GiB; headless Chromium 0.5-1 GiB.

## Rules for agents on this box

- No passwordless `sudo`. If something needs root (apt packages, system config), stop and tell the
  owner the exact command to run.
- User-level installs are fine: Node via nvm (`~/.nvm`), npm globals, `~/.local/bin`, uv.
- Put projects in `~/projects/`; keep `~` itself clean.
- **A repo's own AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md beats this file.** Read it before you touch the repo.
  `django-strawberry-framework` forbids branches and agent commits unless the owner says so, and
  edits only in its `.work/` copies (`scripts/work.py`).
- **Repos in `~/projects/` fast-forward from origin every 3 minutes** (`git-autosync`, a systemd user
  timer) when their tree is clean, not ahead or diverged, no `heavy` job runs in them and no
  `work.py land` or fleet sync holds their `.work/land.lock`. `git-autosync --status` shows what it
  did and why it skipped.
- SSH logins auto-attach to tmux session `main`. Run long jobs in a tmux window, not under `nohup`
  or a bare `&` from a non-interactive shell (the job inherits SIGINT/SIGQUIT as ignored, which breaks
  tests that send signals).
- `agent-status`: one line, "can I start another agent?" (pressure verdict, running agent CLIs, heavy
  slots, free RAM).
